# Yoga Practice

Tamil title: யோகாப்யாசம்

Tears overflow from the young woman's eyes.

As though declaring, "What does this world matter? Above lies the Lord's realm of grace, calling me," the yogi stands with a solemn face.

"He is no yogi, but a man blinded by lust, a demon who violated me. Disguised as a guide to liberation, this fraud deceives the town," cries the young woman. Her voice is soaked more in pain than anger.

The court, however, sees a man who appears to have subdued his senses, rejected worldly pleasure, and sought heavenly bliss: a Yogeswarar with a serene form, eyes flowing with grace, and the power to give humanity many paths to salvation. Would such a man do what even the lowest villain would shrink from? How can it be believed? The court thinks and hesitates.

Yogeswarar possesses enormous influence.

Members of royal families bow at his feet. Many among the upper classes believe prostrating before him brings the merit obtained by worshipping the God of gods in temples.

"I regard this world as no more than dust beneath my feet. My kingdom is not here; there, God calls me," he proclaims.

"Those who perform degraded acts will be punished by God.

"Take up the noble path of austerity.

"Labour for the village; that is the highest penance.

"The Lord waits to grant blessings. Perform good deeds and receive his grace.

"In solitude I converse joyfully with the embodiment of spiritual bliss dwelling in my consciousness. If you follow my words, you too may receive that great fortune."

Thus the Yogeswarar has expounded the Lord's greatness and the moral path.

A woman now says, "He is no yogi but a sensualist, no ascetic but a rogue, no teacher of the path to divine grace but a criminal who violates chastity." Her very voice shows that she has endured cruelty. Looking at his form, however, one feels Yogeswarar could not even imagine such an evil act.

The trial is taking place in Delhi, now.

Do not think him ordinary or merely one among herds of sadhus and renunciants. He is not a wanderer who learnt Vedanta, donned a costume, and sought offerings. He has received God's complete favour. Fool, he is God himself in this form.

Let me explain who he is. He is Gopalakrishna himself: the one who grazed cattle at Ayarpadi, leapt into Kalinga's pool and danced upon the deadly serpent, killed Putana who came to poison him with her milk, climbed the punnai tree, played the flute, and hid the maidens' garments.

We are all gopis, you too. In a former birth we played kolattam and rejoiced with Krishna in Brindavan. This is the Kali age, yet Kannan has come to delight us again. Because we were gopis then, we now have the opportunity to sing, dance, and enjoy ourselves with God.

"Gopika, do not be angry or afraid. God calls; our lord calls. Conduct yourself so as to please him. We may witness the sports of Brindavan again. The mere touch of the dark-cloud-hued one's hand makes the body thrill. Go, girl. He calls to bestow grace.

"Must I explain the bliss of joining face to lotus face and lip to lip in the embrace of that joyous Krishna? Fortunate girl, why tremble? He is our lord, Krishna himself. You are a gopika, and the lover who fondles gopis has come.

"Think your penance fulfilled and go worship the flute-player who has arrived in an ascetic's guise. Millions may offer flowers, but you have obtained the chance to worship him with sweet speech. The hand that lifted Govardhana as an umbrella will comb your hair and pinch your cheek. Who receives such fortune?"

Believing Yogeswarar an incarnation of Krishna, calling him God, and regarding it as a blessing to massage his feet, many worshippers turned every young woman he invited to bed into a "gopika."

Even the disciple who instructed the complainant that this yogi was Krishna belonged to a royal family.

Yet the yogi who possessed a royal disciple would not spare even a ripe young fruit.

The eighteen-year-old came from a refugee family hunted by communal hatred before reaching this "holy land." Her mother lived in tears; her brother kept accounts for the sensualist. She was a young woman. When the yogi assumed responsibility for improving her condition, mother and daughter rejoiced. They thought poverty would stop beating them, suffering would be wiped away, and life would begin.

What did that girl know of the yogi's heart? Had a vigorous young man with a budding moustache and seductive speech said, "I shall protect you," mother and daughter would have recognized the thunderbolt. "No, Appa. We will survive by begging at four houses rather than lose our honour. You, an embodiment of compassion protecting a maiden? We want none of your company," they would have said and driven him away.

But the man offering life was Yogeswarar, one who had realized the world was illusion and taught others. They considered his invitation a blessing and worshipped him with folded hands and tears of gratitude. How could they know that lust hid within while a yogi showed outside?

Youth and beauty trembled before him, an unsupported refugee girl. Yogeswarar's whole mind itched. What a feast, what a delightful opportunity! He took her to the palace of a richly adorned woman who, through blind devotion, believed him Krishna and herself a gopi whenever she received his attentions.

Seeing the young woman, the palace resident rejoiced. "What fortune! Who receives such magnificence? You are blessed to perform 'service' for Krishna himself. It is the fruit of a former birth."

The girl was bewildered. She imagined the holy man had brought her because her family lacked livelihood and support. Perhaps his influence would secure work as a servant in some mansion, place her in an orphanage to learn education and a trade, or enable her to make flower garlands for a temple and live on the wages.

Had Yogeswarar brought her for such work? The sight of her golden body made his heart a surging sea. Her innocent gaze revealed a world of pleasure. Perfect youth, jasmine untouched by a bee.

The "service" he expected was different, but the good young woman did not understand. He cast a devouring look; she wondered why. He called her close, and she came without hesitation. He smiled softly; she grew frightened. He said her body would now become pure gold, asked her age, and urged her to abandon suffering. He asked for fruit and boiled milk. Knowing nothing of the world, she moved with him without suspicion.

The more innocently she behaved, the more monstrous his hunger became. He sighed heavily. She did not seem one who would yield at a touch, yet restraint tormented him. Deciding that the girl did not know life's secret and that he himself must teach it, he turned her into an object of pleasure.

Terrified, she cried, "Ayyo, Swami! What is this? Why do you hold me so tightly that I cannot breathe?"

"Sugar candy, the joy I feel embracing you is the same I felt playing with the gopis in Brindavan. Who are you but a gopi, young woman? I am Gopalakrishna," he coaxed.

So long as this mental delusion survives, bands of mendicants who plan to enjoy whomever they catch will continue their outrages. Can every crime reach a court?

At least once a year, the fraud of some agent of heaven committing such degradation is exposed. Yet the people's delusion does not end.

What do the philosophers who say religion is essential to preserve morality say about this Delhi holy man's fraud? What say the great men who threaten total destruction if people depart from the righteous path and boast that sadhus, renunciants, liberated souls, sages, and those cleansed of the three impurities are ornaments of this holy land?

They may sob that it is shameful and painful that the matter became public. They may wring their hands and say, "Madman, could he not have obtained the woman's consent? Why behave so wildly and bring humiliation upon all of us?" For four days they will look around cautiously. That is all. Who condemned him?

Had an atheist or rationalist committed such an outrage, the whole town would have risen and stoned him to death. Religious leaders would have wiped their eyes and cried in choked voices:

"True devotees, sin has multiplied. The world will perish in a great deluge. Shall we leave in the country great sinners who insult Mahesan, mock divine glory, and preach atheism until their tongues are scarred? Rise today and overthrow the wicked. There is no sin even in killing them; destroying evil is a sacred duty."

But now? What do Kundrakudi and Dharmapuram do? Why are the great and little monasteries silent? What do elegant preachers of morality and holders of power do? Each merely thanks Siva's grace that he himself was not caught. Who comes forward to condemn such vileness?

Not only do they fail to condemn it; they wander seeking and creating ever-new Yogeswarars.

An excuse might be made when ordinary uneducated people cannot detect fraud. But world-travelled dignitaries, experts in government, and heads of scientific institutions become simpletons in this one field.

High-ranking government officials maintained close ties with this monastery. People now say that this emboldened its inmates to violate law and attack society during the monastery disturbance near Cuttack.

A few deceivers turned Cuttack into a camp of gods because they could fool the unthinking.

Only the previous year in this region, hundreds of thousands believed that, through divine grace, a cowherd boy could make tree bark cure every disease. They went to behold the miracle and many died from severe illness. Such was the region's overflowing clarity.

Nor was this confined to Cuttack. Except in our own homeland, rationalist propaganda had not grown strongly in the other states of the Indian subcontinent; ignorance and superstition therefore stank everywhere.

Thinking Cuttack a suitable hunting ground, a mendicant established a monastery. As snakes seek holes in small hills, foxes bushes, and leopards caves, Pagala Baba recognized that this place would nourish his trade. Twenty years earlier he opened the monastery and used the people's blind devotion as capital to build a fortress of fraud.

The enormous monastery stood beside a beautiful river, surrounded by a fine flower garden. Within were not merely halls and upper storeys but many hiding places.

Had Pagala Baba revealed a path no saint had ever taught, offered a new plan for human welfare, or created an epic? No. Such things were ordinary; Pagala Baba was God. The fraudsters' propaganda made people believe this, and they worshipped him as divine.

The monastery possessed its own chariot, festivals, worship, and rituals. Situated south of the Mahanadi in Orissa, it was called Kalibodha Monastery and had countless disciples.

Countless disciples meant offerings piled like hills. The monastery grew fat and Pagala Baba's influence reached its peak. There were two governments in Orissa: the people's government and Mahatma Pagala Baba's. Of the two, the latter possessed overwhelming strength and influence.

The seventy-year-old did not attract devotees seeking instruction or moral knowledge; they gathered to worship him and obtain liberation. Nor were all devotees uneducated. Many who joined or visited Kalibodha held great positions, including senior officers in the Orissa government. The monastery therefore feared nothing.

Pagala Baba's men were mendicants in appearance and villains in character. They worked to increase the monastery's power.

Some said mysterious things happened within, that its men boldly violated the law and showed no respect for government, and that disaster would follow if this continued. No one listened. Since senior officials themselves were disciples, police feared even to watch the monastery.

This impunity increased arrogance, and the monastery's men began committing atrocities.

In a village called Sikarpur, a young man refused Pagala Baba's authority.

"Your father is our disciple," the monastery's men said.

"My intelligence has not died. How could I enter that monastery?" he replied.

They were enraged. The whole country bowed to Pagala Baba, yet this dog dared oppose them. They vowed to teach him the monastery's methods until he fell crying at the Baba's feet for forgiveness.

Do not imagine they used influence with Mahesan to give the dissenter an itching skull, chest pain, ulcer, leprosy, or blindness and thereby reform him.

Like torch-bearing robbers attacking merchants in a forest, monastery men entered the village, assaulted the young man and those who tried to protect him, dragged him away while beating him, and left him half dead at the monastery.

As such outrages grew, the police feared that continued silence would gravely harm society and began action.

For three years they had received information that the monastery secretly accumulated dangerous weapons. Why should a place of grace and worship, meant to teach people to cut away selfishness, arrogance, and desire and learn life's impermanence, purity, truth, morality, and noble character, contain swords, spears, javelins, daggers, guns, and explosives?

Should not a monastery contain incense, offerings, and many kinds of prayer beads? Why weapons in a place where divine men meditated to remove karma and seek grace? What country were they preparing to invade? Why would those fighting only to escape the bonds of the illusory world need instruments of death?

The police had been unable to act for three years. After the monastery men arrogantly entered the village, ignored law, and caused a major disturbance, they decided silence was no longer possible and went to arrest the offenders.

What did they find?

Did they find men singing "Om Namah Sivaya" with devotion, standing on needle points in penance, entering fire pits while chanting Nityananda, grinding medicinal leaves for the sick, gathering fresh milk for infants, or performing bhajans?

No. One expects these in a monastery. A family man may become enchanted by tears, smiles, bangles, sweet speech, or a baby's first steps, forget Mahesan, think this life permanent, and sink into illusion. A monastery is said to disperse family attachment and worldly illusion, create wisdom, and cultivate the ability to seek divine grace.

But what the police saw at Kalibodha shocked them. They found not devotees but criminals. They wore ochre yet shouted wildly. One mendicant drew a sword; many hurled abuse; another impostor held bow and arrow; another threatened them with a spear. In a sacred grove where worship bells should ring came this empty clamour. The monastery band blocked the police.

"Some offenders are hiding here, and we have come to arrest them. Obstructing and attacking us is a grave offence. You wear the form of sadhus and renunciants, yet commit unlawful acts. This is wrong. The law will not spare you," police officers warned.

The mendicants roared, attacked the police, and some fled and hid. The danger exceeded even that of capturing a great gang of robbers. This was called a monastery, yet these were its deeds.

The search was led by an Inspector General whose name is damaged in the source and District Magistrate Ananthakrishnan. Monastery men waylaid and struck them. Police used batons, but the well-fed bodies did not fear and struck back. Many police were injured. Tear-gas shells had no effect. Finally armed police were called, and only after a three-hour struggle could the law be restored and the band of renunciants subdued.

This battle occurred on July 17-18. Two sadhus died, one policeman was killed, and six police were injured: the harvest of the realm of grace.

Police searched the monastery and found weapons in heaps, an entire armoury. Nor only weapons; women were there.

"Who are you, Amma? Why did you come here?"

One passage of her reply is lost to OCR.

"Did I come? They carried me off and brought me here, Ayya. I am married. These sinners..."

How could the eighteen-year-old describe what happened? Five women in similar circumstances sought police protection.

Pagala Baba and the men said to be his right and left hands were now in prison.

A forty-year-old named Bairagi Baba supposedly bore the title of Pagala Baba's successor and had worked vigorously at collecting weapons. Afraid he would have to reveal the truth if caught, he attempted suicide, but police prevented it and imprisoned him. He had reportedly been dismissed from the police, joined the monastery five or six years earlier, and prepared to become the next "God."

An example of how foolishly senior officials were caught by these frauds was that one arrestee, a deputy senior officer in a government department, bore the OCR-damaged name Sakananajina.

People were told Pagala Baba was divine, and believed it. They were told Mahesan's grace would flow if they entered the monastery, and said yes.

Nor was that all. In Cuttack city, a story was fabricated that a certain woman was an incarnation of Kali. People believed that too.

Even in these days of immense intellectual growth, Kalibodha Monastery could exist. Fortresses of jungle rule acquired enough arrogance to challenge elected governments. They wore ochre and violated women. They held water pots filled with the tears of ravaged maidens.

This "yoga practice" of religious fanatics who erased people's reason and committed great crimes continued without obstruction.

Listen to their songs dripping with devotion. Look at the sacred ash they wear, making them appear as ripe embodiments of Siva. Hear their gracious instruction, enough to melt flesh and heart. Go inside, and you will see Bairagi Baba and the beautiful women ruined in the hands of men like him.

We lack the strength to uproot such injustice, the power to suppress it, and even a mouth to condemn it. Yet the Parabrahmans who live like emperors in monasteries funded by public offerings complain that we insult them as atheists.

Speak to them and honey flows. Ask them to conduct a philosophical enquiry and one feels disgust at carrying so degraded a human birth and considers retiring to the forest for penance. How extensively they have sung of this wretched body's degradation:

A forest where five thieves riot;

A chest of filth;

A casket of twin karma;

A little town inhabited by wind, bile, and phlegm;

A strip of wounded skin ordered to decay;

A useless fragment;

A garden of demon gourds;

A top dancing on the cord of desire;

A vessel bearing ceaseless disease;

A cage of illusion, change, and death;

A sack for rice;

A block cut by Yama along the line of fate;

A shirt stitched by the four-faced Brahma.

That description is for common people. The commodity required by the mendicants who sing it is an eighteen-year-old maiden.

When the late Kali N. Rathnam said, "Swamis, shall we proceed to yoga practice?" in the film *Chandrakanta*, religious controversialists, monastery heads, and defenders of the faith howled and created an uproar. Many years have passed since that film faded away, but yoga practice continues today. Who is there to stop it?

Before rational thought spreads, who will possess that courage? The holy men who tremble at spreading rationalism and lament that morality and culture will perish: what do they say about the "yoga practice" in Delhi and Cuttack?

Two offenders were caught. Countless others continue their yoga practice uncaught.

Do you wish to know where? Search with the eye of reason. They exist all around us.

The common people lie enchanted before them. The wealthy pour offerings upon them. Government officials seek refuge there. Maidens are violated for the single "crime" of possessing beauty.

Yoga practice continues, friends. It continues.

8 August 1954

---NOTES---
The title is verified from repeated later occurrences as “யோகாப்யாசம்” (yoga practice); the opening scan reads “பயோகாப்யாசம்,” an apparent OCR error. The phrase is used throughout as bitter irony for sexual exploitation and institutional violence, not literal yoga instruction.
